<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Romans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman Empire was the state ruled by emperors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, at its height controlling the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world and much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Its western half ended with the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> in 476 CE, while the eastern half continued as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. It serves as a reference point across many profiles, including those of the Carolingian and Holy Roman empires, which claimed its legacy, and its rivals the Parthian and Punic empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-empire.jpg" alt="Roman Empire" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tataryn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_Trajan_117AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Africa Proconsularis, built on the ruins of Punic Carthage, was one of the wealthiest and most urbanised regions of the <b>Roman Empire</b>, feeding Rome with grain and oil, supplying an emperor and senators, and nurturing the writers and controversies that shaped Latin Christianity from Tertullian to Augustine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the first states to adopt Christianity officially and the first to place the cross on its coinage, decades before Christianity became the official religion of the <b>Roman Empire</b> under Theodosius I, and established the Ethiopian Orthodox tradition as one of the oldest continuous Christian institutions in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He cooperated with Rome against Antiochus III and rebuilt his kingdom's finances and manpower, but his reign ended in bitterness after he had his younger son Demetrius, a favourite of the <b>Romans</b>, executed in 180 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sulla besieged the city and stormed it on 1 March 86 BCE, ending its independent political life, although Athens survived as a privileged free city and university town of the <b>Roman Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The fundamental weakness was the empire's relationship with its subject peoples: unlike the Achaemenid Persians or the <b>Romans</b>, the Aztecs made little systematic effort to integrate conquered populations into a shared imperial identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharnaces II, Asander and his wife Dynamis, Polemon I and then Aspurgus ruled with Roman approval, and during the first century CE the kings took the names Tiberius Julius, styled themselves friends of Caesar and of the <b>Romans</b>, and placed the emperor’s portrait on their coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
